How to keep discovering indie publishers adopting audiobooks

To systematically find more independent publishers converting their catalogues into audiobooks, consider the following strategies:

Search audiobook platforms directly – Platforms like Audible, Libro.fm, Storytel, Apple Books and Spotify often allow filtering by publisher. Regularly search these sites for new or small presses. Pay attention to publisher pages, as they confirm audio releases.

Use targeted search phrases – Combine publisher names or generic terms with keywords such as “audiobook rights,” “audio edition,” “audio imprint,” “partnered with [audio company],” or “released as audiobook.” Searching for press releases or news articles (e.g., “independent publisher launches audiobook program”) can reveal announcements
publishingperspectives.com
.

Monitor trade news and newsletters – Industry publications like Publishers Weekly, The Bookseller, Publishing Perspectives and AudioFile frequently report on indie presses entering the audio space. Set up alerts for “audiobook” plus “independent publisher” or specific regions.

Check publishers’ websites and social media – Indie presses often announce audio deals on their own news pages, blogs or Twitter feeds. Look for sections titled “audiobooks,” “audio rights,” or “audio editions,” as seen with Muswell Press and Serpent’s Tail
muswell-press.co.uk
serpentstail.com
.

Explore distributor catalogues – Digital distributors (Bookwire, RBmedia, Saga Egmont, Dreamscape, Bolinda, W.F. Howes) list partner publishers on their sites. These lists highlight presses that have recently signed audio deals.

Investigate grants and university programs – Nonprofit presses and university presses sometimes receive grants to convert titles to audio (e.g., New Directions with Deyan Audio
publishersweekly.com
). Search for “university press audiobook program” to uncover similar initiatives.

Watch for rights announcements – Publishers often announce that they have acquired “world audio rights” or similar. Tracking rights newsletters or the “deals” sections of trade magazines can surface new opportunities
publishing.hardiegrant.com
.

Using these methods will help you stay ahead of the curve and identify indie publishers who are embracing audiobooks—ideal prospects for your streamlined audio‑production service.